    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cells must monitor and respond to chemical and physical changes in their environments. For example, swelling or shrinking in response to osmotic changes presents both chemical and physical stimuli. Mechanisms of sensing and responding to these stimuli fall into two broad categories: signal transducers and safety valves. Bacteria utilize the safety valve mechanism, as exemplified by the protein MscS (mechanosensitive channel of small conductance). A family of MscS-like (MSL) proteins has been identified in plants, including Arabidopsis thaliana. Several MSLs have been previously characterized, for example, MSL9 and MSL10 have been shown to respond to membrane stretching. By constructing fluorescent biosensors based on these MSLs, we aimed to design a sensor which reports on changes in membrane tension. Several such chimeric proteins have been constructed based on MSL9 and MSL10, and their response to hyperosmotic osmotic stress has been characterized in Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Surprisingly, we discovered that sensor expression in yeast leads to aggregation, and closer inspection of the aggregates reveals that the majority of the signal is derived from these aggregates. These sensor aggregates could be used to detect osmotic changes in yeast and other systems, however, these aggregates do not report on membrane tension at the cell surface. Future work with these sensors includes reducing aggregation in yeast systems as well as improving surface targeting. Applications include using these novel sensors for in vivo visualizations of membrane stress in physiological conditions, such as plant roots encountering a barrier, or the effect of membrane stress on growth and development.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
